public static String removeChar(String s, char c) {
    if (s.length() == 0) {
        return "";
    }
    if (s.charAt(0) == c) {
        return removeChar(s.substring(1), c);
    }
    return s.charAt(0) + removeChar(s.substring(1), c);
}

public static int findFirst(int[] a, int t, int lo, int hi) {
    if (lo > hi) {
        return -1;
    }
    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
    if (a[mid] < t) {
        return findFirst(a, t, mid + 1, hi);
    }
    if (a[mid] > t) {
        return findFirst(a, t, lo, mid - 1);
    }
    int left = findFirst(a, t, lo, mid - 1);
    if (left != -1) {
        return left;
    }
    return mid;
}
```
When `a[mid]` equals `t`, the first occurrence is either `mid` itself or somewhere to its left, so the method searches `a[lo..mid - 1]` and falls back on `mid` if nothing is found there.

```java
public static int ways(int n) {
    if (n < 0) {
        return 0;
    }
    if (n == 0) {
        return 1;
    }
    return ways(n - 1) + ways(n - 2) + ways(n - 3);
}

public static boolean canMake(int[] vals, int index, int target) {
    if (target == 0) {
        return true;
    }
    if (index >= vals.length) {
        return false;
    }
    return canMake(vals, index + 1, target - vals[index]) || canMake(vals, index + 1, target);
}
```
In `ways`, the last move is 1, 2 or 3 steps, so the number of ways to climb `n` steps is the sum of the numbers of ways to climb `n - 1`, `n - 2` and `n - 3` steps. In `canMake`, each value is either used (the target shrinks by that value) or skipped.
